# R503 Optical Fingerprint Module [SPEC:CXC-R503]

> The R503 is the peripheral the transactional HAL exists for. The facts below live BELOW the HAL: they are
> the part's electrical and protocol realities, several of which are hazards a naive driver or board will
> walk into. Each is a tight, citable truth; the R503 backend is extended to MEET the agnostic contract, it
> is never forked around.

---

## The part

### [CXC-R503-PART] Six pins, two independent rails, a 200-template store, an on-chip matcher

- interface: UART, 3.3V TTL logic level, default 57600 baud.
- capacity: 200 templates, consistent across all four manual revisions.
- on-chip-matcher: the module performs both 1:1 match (0x03 Match) and 1:N search (0x04 Search) itself; matching is sensor-side.
- template-size: 1536 bytes on V1.2.1 onward; V1.1 firmware said 768 bytes. The size is announced through the HAL, never compiled as a constant (CXC-FPHAL-CAPABILITIES-SCHEMA).
- ring-led: a 7-colour, 6-mode addressable ring driven by 0x35 AuraLedConfig.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]
- see-also: [CXC-R503-PART-IDENTITY-RISK], [CXC-R503-AURA-LED]

### [CXC-R503-PART-IDENTITY-RISK] Part identity is unconfirmed — four sibling parts differ on rail voltage and connector

- hazard: four parts sell as "R503-something" and marketplace listings conflate them: R503 (3.3V), R503-5V (5V rail, MX1.25-6P connector), R503S and R503Pro. Every figure in this document is an R503 figure; applied to an R503-5V they are wrong in the direction that destroys the part, whose pin 1 is DC 5V.
- rule: confirm the part from the physical label and the listing FIRST, then from the wire via ReadProdInfo 0x3C (returns PARAM_FPM_MODEL as 16 ASCII bytes) once it is safely powered. Choosing a rail requires knowing the part; reading the wire requires powering it, so the label leads.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

---

## Board hazards — rails, pinout, and the reference circuit

### [CXC-R503-PINOUT] Six pins on a captive pigtail — pin 1 is photo-identified and wire colour means nothing

- pins: 1 VCC (DC3.3V), 2 GND, 3 TXD (out, TTL), 4 RXD (in, TTL), 5 WAKEUP (finger detect, standby HIGH, finger LOW), 6 3.3VT (touch supply, DC3-5V).
- pin-1-evidence: pin 1 and pin 6 ends are identified only by a photograph; there is no keying drawing, mechanical outline or polarity mark in the text.
- trap-wire-colour: the manual states flatly that line order has nothing to do with colour. NEVER infer pinout from wire colour on this part.
- connector-is-on-the-cable: the connector is on a captive pigtail, not the module body, so the designation describes the free end of the cable and a PCB needs the mating half.
- rule: measure the physical connector on the part in hand before committing any footprint; the connector part number drifts across revisions (CXC-R503-REVISION-DRIFT).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-RAILS] Two independent rails, an unregulated input, no hot-plug, a two-second wake floor

- vcc: pin 1, DC 3.3V, a point value with no tolerance band stated in any revision.
- vt: pin 6, DC3-5V (V1.1 said DC3-6V, silently narrowed).
- rails-are-independent: the low-power mode cuts VCC while keeping 3.3VT on, and the module still detects a finger and signals WAKEUP. This is the ground of the WAKEUP hazard below.
- input-is-unregulated: power is supplied directly to the image sensor and decoding chip, so board-side filtering is the host's job; the ripple figure is a recommendation, not a limit.
- no-hot-plug: components may be damaged by inserting or removing the cable while it is live.
- power-cycle-dwell: after power-off the part must stay off at least two seconds before power-on. If VCC is power-gated, this is a hard floor on wake latency and dominates the ~50 ms init time.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]
- see-also: [CXC-R503-WAKEUP-TRACKS-3V3T]

### [CXC-R503-WAKEUP-TRACKS-3V3T] WAKEUP's high level very likely tracks the 3.3VT touch rail, so 3.3VT is tied to 3V3

- claim: the WAKEUP output's HIGH level tracks the 3.3VT rail, not VCC.
- evidence-class: STRONG INFERENCE, not a datasheet fact, and it must not be softened. The manual says the touch signal output is CMOS and roughly the same as the input voltage; it never writes "the same as 3.3VT".
- ground-one: the sentence sits inside a paragraph entirely about the touch supply.
- ground-two: the low-power mode requires WAKEUP to work while VCC is off, which is only possible if WAKEUP is powered from 3.3VT.
- hazard: feeding 3.3VT at 5V — which the manual permits — very likely puts a ~5V CMOS high on WAKEUP. On a 3.3V, non-5V-tolerant ESP32 GPIO that is an overdrive.
- rule: TIE 3.3VT TO 3.3V unless and until measured otherwise. Do not wire WAKEUP straight to a bare GPIO without confirming its high level.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]
- see-also: [CXC-R503-RAILS], [CXC-R503-REFERENCE-CIRCUIT]

### [CXC-R503-REFERENCE-CIRCUIT] The only schematic is for the 5V R307 — building it as drawn puts 5V on pin 1

- hazard: the sole schematic in the manual (section VII) is labelled R307 with pin 1 = 5V, and the manual says so plainly under the drawing. A reader wanting a known-good power-gating circuit finds exactly one, follows it, and lands 5V on the R503's 3.3V point-value rail.
- the-useful-lesson: the vendor does NOT wire TOUCH straight to a GPIO. It buffers TOUCH into a power-latch path — an independent design answer to the WAKEUP overdrive risk, arrived at by the people who made the part.
- rule: do not build the reference circuit as drawn for an R503; reuse only the topology lesson (buffer TOUCH, do not drive a GPIO from it directly).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

---

## The wire protocol, and the three ways it breaks request/response

### [CXC-R503-FRAMING] Big-endian, address-gated, and Length counts the checksum

- shape: [Header 2B = 0xEF01][Address 4B][PID 1B][Length 2B][Content ...][Checksum 2B].
- byte-order: big-endian throughout (high byte first). This statement is absent from V1.1, so a V1.1-era driver had no byte-order statement at all.
- address: default 0xFFFFFFFF; a wrong address makes the module reject the transfer with SILENCE, not an error packet.
- pid: 01H command, 02H data, 07H acknowledge, 08H end-of-data. A data packet must follow a command or acknowledge packet, never appear alone.
- length-semantics: Length = content bytes plus 2 (it includes the checksum). A parser that reads Length as the content length overruns by two bytes on every packet.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]
- see-also: [CXC-R503-CHECKSUM]

### [CXC-R503-CHECKSUM] The checksum rule, recomputed against 21 worked examples and passing

- rule: the checksum is the arithmetic sum of PID, both Length bytes, and every content byte, modulo 2^16. Header and address are NOT included.
- verification: recomputed against all 21 worked examples in the manual set (18 fixed-command checksums plus three V1.4.1 hex traces); all 21 recompute exactly.
- verify-machine: a unit test that recomputes the checksum for the AuraLedConfig example EF01 FFFFFFFF 01 0007 35 01 50 06 00 yields 0x0094. This runs against a byte string, touches no hardware, and is not a wire probe.
- caution: never author a wrong-checksum or wrong-length probe against a real part; a malformed frame is a potential brick vector and there is no documented recovery path.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-UNSOLICITED-0X55] The module emits a bare unframed 0x55 at power-on AND after every soft reset

- behaviour: the module sends a single unframed byte 0x55 to the host, unsolicited — the only unsolicited emission in the protocol. It fires at power-on and again after 0x3D SoftRst.
- hazard: it is a bare byte with no header, address, length or checksum. A fixed-length-frame parser is permanently phase-shifted from that byte; a driver that absorbs it only at boot desyncs the first time it soft-resets.
- remedy: drain RX immediately before every transmit AND parse with a sliding two-byte header window. Either alone is partial; together they absorb the power-on byte, the post-SoftRst byte, and any stale packet from an aborted transaction.
- verify-machine: against a mock that emits a leading 0x55, assert the driver's first command still receives its framed acknowledgement. The mock, not a part, produces the stray byte.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-ACK-STREAM-BREAKS-REQ-RESP] Auto commands emit acknowledge STREAMS, and the enabling bit differs per command

- behaviour: with the status-reporting config bit set, 0x31 AutoEnroll emits fifteen acknowledge packets from one command (step 0x01 through 0x0F, only 0x0F carrying the modelID); 0x32 AutoIdentify emits three.
- hazard-config-index: the enabling parameter is at a DIFFERENT index on each command — Config3 on AutoEnroll, Config1 on AutoIdentify. A driver that generalises "Config3 enables streaming" mis-configures AutoIdentify.
- rule: a one-response-per-command transaction assumption is wrong for these commands; the HAL must treat them as bounded streams terminated by confirmation != 0x00 or by the final step code, not by a single reply.
- version-hazard: V1.2.1's AutoEnroll step codes are numerically incoherent and were fixed in V1.4; a driver written against V1.2.1's step table mis-sequences on current firmware.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]
- see-also: [CXC-R503-AUTOIDENTIFY-STREAM-UNBOUNDED]

### [CXC-R503-AUTOIDENTIFY-STREAM-UNBOUNDED] AutoIdentify's stream can be unbounded — bound it in the driver

- claim: with a finger continuously present that never matches and the retry count set to zero, 0x32 AutoIdentify loops until a match or power-off, so its acknowledge stream is unbounded. This is an inference from the stated retry semantics; the manual never shows a retry trace.
- why-the-timeout-does-not-save-you: the 10-to-15-second no-finger exit bounds the finger-ABSENT case, not the finger-present-never-matches case — exactly the case a confused or hostile user produces.
- rule: never size a fixed ring buffer on the assumption the stream is short; the HAL match operation must impose its own bound and surface a PROTOCOL_DESYNC or a bounded no-match rather than blocking.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

---

## Feedback and timing

### [CXC-R503-AURA-LED] Seven colours, six modes, a five-second minimum animation cycle

- command: 0x35 AuraLedConfig, payload [Ctrl][Speed][ColorIndex][Times].
- colours: seven — Red, Blue, Purple, Green, Yellow, Cyan, White (indices 0x01 through 0x07). A three-colour driver loses four of them.
- modes: breathing, flashing, always-on, always-off, gradual-on, gradual-off.
- the-five-second-floor: the animator's minimum cycle is five seconds, so it cannot express a fast acknowledgement blink. Any sub-second feedback MUST be realized as host-timed always-on / always-off transitions, never the module's own animator.
- old-firmware-risk: V1.1 firmware documents only three colours (Red, Blue, Purple); Green first appears in V1.2.1. On a part whose firmware predates V1.2.1 an accept-green mapping may be unimplementable, which is a game-design ruling to make, not a workaround to code around. Ten seconds on a bench settles which colours the silicon drives.
- reject-silent-invariant: the realization MUST render reject_silent identically to a genuine no_match — same colour, mode, speed and bytes — so a player cannot learn the tell. This binds on the R503 mapping table, not on the agnostic feedback enum (CXC-FPHAL-FEEDBACK-STATE-SET).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-MATCH-CHEAP-CAPTURE-EXPENSIVE] Imaging dominates the verify budget — capture is the cost, not the match

- claim: a one-shot verify budget is roughly image acquisition (<0.2s) plus feature generation (<0.5s) plus the search, dominated by imaging. The manual states no per-command match latency and the 1:N string "1:N<10ms/Fingerprint" is ambiguous by 200x, but at the fleet's ceiling of twenty resident templates the worst reading is ~200 ms, so sensor-side matching wins under both readings and capture is the cost.
- evidence-class: INFERENCE — arithmetic on separately specified worst cases the manual never sums. It is an order-of-magnitude expectation, not a budget, and must not be restated as a specification.
- consequence: optimising the matcher buys almost nothing; optimising the capture path buys almost everything. It also confirms matching belongs on the part rather than in a central matcher.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

