## Revision drift and the baud brick-risk

### [CXC-R503-REVISION-DRIFT] Physically consequential figures drift silently; the changelog records none of them

- claim: the connector part number (MX1.0-6P to SH1.0-6P), the 3.3VT range (6V to 5V), the template sizes (768/384 to 1536/512), the boot time, the ring-LED colour count (three to seven), the AutoEnroll step-code renumbering and the default security level all changed between revisions, and the manual's own revision table records none of them.
- rule: do not use the changelog to decide whether a revision matters to you; it is materially incomplete in the direction that hurts. Diff the figures you depend on across revisions directly.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-BAUD-RATE] Baud stays at the 57600 default — a baud change can brick the part with no recovery

- hazard: every revision contradicts itself on legal baud rates (an intro table implying N=1..6, a control section enumerating N in {1,2,4,6,12}, and a prose range 9600-115200bps). A widely-used driver, faithfully implementing the range sentence, exposes seven forbidden baud constants; setting one and power-cycling can leave the module at an unreachable baud rate with NO documented recovery path.
- rule: the HAL binding pins baud at 57600, the documented default, which needs no SetSysPara call. The safest driver never issues a baud change. Do not probe baud rates on a module you care about; a brick has no recovery.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

