## The platform binding

### [CXC-R503-HAL-BINDING] The transactional HAL declaration the R503 realization meets

- declaration-id: bioscan
- declaration-role: transactional
- declaration-driver: r503
- declaration-interface: uart baud 57600
- declaration-assurance: experiential
- why-transactional: a sensor publishes and an actuator subscribes; neither can express an enrollment that takes six finger presentations over several seconds and yields a modelID. The transactional role exists for exactly this part.
- what-it-means-here: the R503 driver is one realization of the agnostic HAL (SPEC:CXC-FPHAL). It is extended to meet that contract; the contract is never forked per backend.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]
- see-also: [CXC-R503-ASSURANCE]

### [CXC-R503-ASSURANCE] The sensor is experiential, not access control

- claim: the fingerprint match is an experiential assurance class — it makes a game ceremony feel real. It is NOT a security control and MUST NOT be relied on as one.
- consequence: the kill-switch that bounds template-network exposure (CXC-FPID-TIER-LIBRARY) bounds an accident, not an adversary; and a device announcing its own gated capability (CXC-FPBUS-DD-CAPABILITY-BINDING) is acceptable because nothing here is defending against a determined attacker.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

